Fix IPC event signal-ring indexing in IPCEventEmulated (hip_event_ipc.cpp).

read_index and write_index in ihipIpcEventShmem_t are absolute, ever-increasing
ring counters, not slot indices.  Upstream added ihipSignalSlot() to map them
into [0, IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT) before they are used to address signal[]; this
backports that helper and its use at all four call sites.  It closes three
defects, not one:

1. streamWait() used the counter verbatim:
       const int offset = ipc_evt_.ipc_shmem_->read_index;
   After 32 uses of the same IPC event handle it names signal[32], outside the
   32 slots that createIpcEventShmemIfNeeded() pins for device access with
   ihipHostRegister(&shmem->signal, sizeof(uint32_t) * IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT).
   The WAIT_VALUE op is then rejected with hipErrorInvalidValue -- the failure
   LMCache MP hits on the 33rd cross-process event import.

2. query() and streamWait() ignored the "nothing recorded yet" sentinel.
   createIpcEventShmemIfNeeded() initialises read_index = -1, so a consumer
   that queried or streamWait()ed before the producer's first record addressed
   signal[-1] (query() applied % to a negative value; streamWait() applied
   nothing at all) -- 4 bytes below the registered region, inside the
   alignas(64) padding of ihipIpcEventShmem_t.  ihipSignalSlot() maps -1 to
   slot 31, a valid zero-initialised word.  synchronize() keeps its explicit
   `prev_read_idx >= 0` early-out, exactly as upstream has it.

3. The ring-full comparisons added IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT to a 32-bit counter;
   widening with int64_t{...} removes the signed-overflow UB after ~2^31
   records.  Note this does NOT change read_index's initialisation: -1 is
   load-bearing for the publication CAS in enqueueRecordCommand(), which
   expects `write_index - 1`, and for synchronize()'s guard.  Do not "fix" the
   overflow by initialising read_index to 0.

Upstream: ROCm/rocm-systems a3e8d96073 (PR #12230, "fix(clr): Adding
%IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT in IPCEventEmulated::streamWait", 2026-09-25) landed the
step-1 one-liner; the ihipSignalSlot() refactor with steps 2 and 3 is on
develop.  Neither is in any tagged ROCm release yet: release/therock-7.14
(= therock-7.14.1, ca887ee), 10.0 and 10.1 are all still unpatched, and the
oldest dated BKC carrying the fix is release/bkc/therock-10.2-20261013.  The
PRs docker/base/Dockerfile cites, #11511 and #11749, were never merged (open
and closed-unmerged respectively).  Drop this patch when the stack moves to a
10.2+ base.

Supersedes patches/hip-ipc-event-ring-fix.patch, which carried only the
step-1 modulo; applying both conflicts.  Both aic-vllm and aic-lmcache must be
rebuilt from the same aic-base: the ring indices are a cross-process protocol,
so a patched consumer paired with an unpatched peer waits on signal[0] while
the producer armed signal[32], which hangs instead of erroring.

Apply from the rocm-systems checkout root (patch -p1), i.e. replace the
`sed -i` in the build_clr_patch stage of docker/base/Dockerfile with:

    patch -p1 -d /app/rocm-systems < /tmp/hip-ipc-signal-slot-index-fix.patch

--- a/projects/clr/hipamd/src/hip_event_ipc.cpp
+++ b/projects/clr/hipamd/src/hip_event_ipc.cpp
@@ -59,14 +59,21 @@ bool IPCEventEmulated::createIpcEventShmemIfNeeded() {
   return status == hipSuccess;
 }
 
+// Indices live in shared memory (a peer can write any value) and start at -1; % keeps the
+// sign, so map into [0, N). Arithmetic on them is done in 64 bits so it can't overflow.
+static inline int ihipSignalSlot(int idx) {
+  const int slot = idx % IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT;
+  return slot < 0 ? slot + IPC_SIGNALS_PER_EVENT : slot;
+}
